62,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, I agree. A is impossible due to saturated glasshouses. C is also risky; the spectroscopy suite is unreliable. We must avoid both.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, C’s simulation costs are now free, offsetting the hardware risk. B’s animal rates have spiked, making it financially untenable.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I agree B is financially risky. But C’s hardware is unreliable. D’s field sites are weather-prone. A is booked solid. We are stuck.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, D is not stuck. The repository now clears deposits in weeks, not years. This catalogue work endures, unlike the stalled mechanism studies.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shizue, the repository speed helps, but D still requires field collection. The weather risks remain. We need a project that avoids external site dependencies entirely.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, D’s field collection is a one-time event. The analysis is indoor. The spectroscopy suite is irrelevant to sequencing. D is the only viable option.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, precisely. D’s collection is a one-time cost, while the analysis leverages free computing. It avoids the spiked animal rates and the saturated glasshouses entirely.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Victoria, I agree. D’s one-time collection is manageable, and the indoor analysis avoids the weather risks I found. It fits our constraints best.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Victoria, I am glad you see it clearly. D’s lasting catalogue value aligns perfectly with what the closeout files taught us about enduring utility.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Victoria, I accept the one-time collection risk. The indoor analysis phase aligns with our current staffing reality. D is the only option that fits.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, I stand by my assessment. D avoids the saturated glasshouses and the broken spectroscopy suite. It is the only proposal that fits our current infrastructure reality.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, agreed. D’s one-time field cost is manageable, unlike B’s recurring animal fees. The free computing makes the analysis phase financially sound.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, I agree. D’s lasting catalogue value aligns with the closeout files. It avoids the weather risks and hardware failures I found. It is the most resilient choice.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"I am content. D offers the lasting utility we seek, free from the infrastructure traps that plague the others. I vote for D.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,I accept the logic. D fits our staffing and infrastructure constraints best. I vote for D.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
